WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:04.840
Imagine for a second that you are typing out an incredibly furious email to your boss.

00:05.540 --> 00:07.780
Oh man, the worst idea.

00:08.140 --> 00:10.200
Right? I'm talking about a real bridge burner.

00:10.620 --> 00:10.820
Yeah.

00:11.360 --> 00:17.780
You hit the send button, but in a sudden blinding flash of panic, a fraction of a millisecond later,

00:18.000 --> 00:20.580
you physically rip the power cord out of the wall.

00:20.920 --> 00:22.080
Just total desperation.

00:22.400 --> 00:27.460
Exactly. The screen goes completely black, the fans spin down, the room is silent.

00:27.460 --> 00:31.700
Now, here is the million dollar question for you.

00:32.800 --> 00:34.280
Did that email send?

00:34.880 --> 00:35.420
Or are you fired?

00:35.960 --> 00:39.060
Yes. Or are you fired? Or did you manage to stop it in time?

00:39.680 --> 00:42.760
Well, it's the ultimate Schrodinger's employment situation.

00:43.260 --> 00:48.620
You don't actually know the state of your reality until you plug that machine back in and check the sent folder.

00:48.620 --> 00:56.000
Right. And the answer to that question relies entirely on a concept that we interact with every single day,

00:56.000 --> 01:00.320
usually without ever really thinking about it. Irreversibility.

01:00.960 --> 01:06.340
Yeah. It's the fundamental physics of the undo button, or really the lack of one.

01:06.800 --> 01:12.040
Exactly. When a system makes a permanent choice, like a choice that alters history,

01:12.840 --> 01:16.660
at what exact microsecond does that choice become real?

01:16.660 --> 01:21.980
It's fascinating because we usually expect reality to operate with, like, the precision of a medical x-ray.

01:22.200 --> 01:22.300
Right.

01:22.420 --> 01:27.220
You know, you break an arm, the x-ray shows a clean, jagged white line, and a doctor can point to it and say,

01:27.320 --> 01:31.680
well, there it is. That is the historical event. This bone was whole, and now it is broken.

01:31.980 --> 01:34.360
We want that binary diagnostic clarity.

01:34.640 --> 01:35.780
Exactly. We crave it.

01:35.780 --> 01:41.140
But we want to be able to look at the universe, or a computer, or a human brain,

01:41.380 --> 01:45.280
and see a perfect, unblemished record of exactly what happened to it.

01:45.440 --> 01:48.780
But the reality is that the universe doesn't always keep the receipts.

01:49.000 --> 01:49.980
No, it really doesn't.

01:50.260 --> 01:54.440
Once you step out of the realm of, like, simple physics and into the world of complex systems,

01:54.560 --> 01:59.440
whether we're talking about the flow of time itself, the architecture of global computer networks,

01:59.440 --> 02:04.760
or even just your own fragile, reconstructive memory, that x-ray machine is utterly useless.

02:04.760 --> 02:06.400
Yeah, it completely breaks down.

02:06.680 --> 02:08.780
The diagnostic landscape just becomes murky.

02:09.480 --> 02:12.440
Because you can't always just look at a system and read its history like an open book.

02:12.720 --> 02:16.780
It is the absolute definition of diagnostic muddy waters.

02:17.300 --> 02:22.400
And, you know, the core reason for that murkiness is that making a permanent mark on reality,

02:22.680 --> 02:27.060
what we might call committing a state to history, it actually costs something.

02:27.380 --> 02:29.660
You can't hit an undo button on reality for free.

02:29.940 --> 02:30.420
Precisely.

02:31.220 --> 02:34.440
Which brings us perfectly to our mission for this deep dive.

02:34.760 --> 02:36.860
And welcome, by the way, to everyone listening.

02:37.720 --> 02:43.820
Today, you and I are exploring a dense, fascinating, and honestly mind-bending monograph from November 2026.

02:44.060 --> 02:45.080
Such a good piece of work.

02:45.300 --> 02:46.040
It really is.

02:46.120 --> 02:51.300
It's by the independent researcher Flyxion, and it's titled Distinction Under Constraint.

02:51.440 --> 02:53.200
Distinction Under Constraint.

02:53.200 --> 02:53.640
Yeah.

02:53.960 --> 02:56.180
And it asks a massive fundamental question.

02:56.920 --> 03:06.620
How do distinct things, you know, objects, identities, decisions, memories, how do they arise and persist in a universe where you literally cannot take anything back?

03:06.620 --> 03:08.920
It is an incredibly ambitious text.

03:09.060 --> 03:14.260
I mean, it spans theoretical physics, computer science, cognitive psychology, and decision theory.

03:14.580 --> 03:15.780
It's all over the map.

03:15.940 --> 03:16.440
It is.

03:16.560 --> 03:25.300
But the brilliance of it is that the underlying math, the actual mechanics of irreversibility, they are exactly the same across every single one of those disciplines.

03:25.300 --> 03:26.660
Which is wild to think about.

03:26.820 --> 03:27.260
Which, yeah.

03:28.000 --> 03:34.580
Whether you are trying to understand why time only moves forward, or whether you're designing a foolproof operating system.

03:34.780 --> 03:35.040
Right.

03:35.380 --> 03:41.600
Or wondering why you misremembered your own childhood, or just trying to make a high-stakes life decision with terrible information.

03:42.260 --> 03:43.280
The mechanics are identical.

03:43.500 --> 03:45.520
It all comes back to the exact same math.

03:45.700 --> 03:45.920
Okay.

03:46.180 --> 03:47.080
Let's unpack this.

03:47.080 --> 03:51.520
Because before we can talk about the cosmos, or the human mind, we have to lay the groundwork.

03:51.660 --> 03:51.820
Yeah.

03:51.940 --> 03:52.860
We have to start at the bottom.

03:53.320 --> 03:58.320
We have to define what it actually means to make a distinction or a commitment in a world with no takebacks.

03:59.340 --> 04:03.480
So, what is a distinction, according to this research?

04:03.920 --> 04:10.100
Well, the author defines a distinction quite elegantly as a difference that makes a difference.

04:10.620 --> 04:12.020
A difference that makes a difference.

04:12.020 --> 04:12.380
Right.

04:12.560 --> 04:16.820
I know that sounds a bit like a circular riddle at first, but it's vital.

04:17.080 --> 04:24.100
We tend to walk around assuming that objects and identities are just, you know, natively there.

04:24.480 --> 04:24.800
Right.

04:24.860 --> 04:28.100
Like the universe is basically a giant bucket filled with distinct things.

04:28.260 --> 04:30.460
Planets, chairs, people, atoms.

04:30.960 --> 04:33.100
Just sitting there waiting for us to observe them.

04:33.520 --> 04:36.280
But the text aggressively rejects that presupposition.

04:36.980 --> 04:40.460
Instead, it treats objects and identities as outcomes.

04:40.900 --> 04:41.300
Outcomes.

04:41.600 --> 04:41.800
Yeah.

04:42.120 --> 04:42.880
They are achievements.

04:43.700 --> 04:45.580
So things aren't just naturally distinct.

04:45.960 --> 04:46.400
Not at all.

04:46.400 --> 04:47.580
So think about it this way.

04:47.940 --> 04:54.180
In a purely theoretical, perfectly reversible system, you could draw a boundary between two things.

04:54.500 --> 04:56.580
Decide you don't like how it looks and just erase it.

04:57.040 --> 04:57.920
It costs you nothing.

04:58.140 --> 04:58.820
It leaves no trace.

04:59.020 --> 05:01.540
But we do not live in a reversible system.

05:01.640 --> 05:01.760
Right.

05:02.160 --> 05:05.440
In an irreversible system, trying something leaves a mark.

05:06.180 --> 05:08.480
Drawing a boundary actually spends physical resources.

05:08.620 --> 05:09.420
It generates heat.

05:09.820 --> 05:11.120
It alters the state of the board.

05:11.120 --> 05:15.480
The act of separating this from that becomes a permanent historical event.

05:16.300 --> 05:18.840
So a distinction is basically a scar.

05:18.840 --> 05:19.740
Oh, wow.

05:20.100 --> 05:20.240
Yeah.

05:20.380 --> 05:24.060
You've carved a line into reality, and now reality has to carry that line forward forever.

05:24.500 --> 05:28.560
That is a very visceral way to put it, but it's conceptually entirely accurate.

05:28.560 --> 05:35.420
And this leads to the core methodology of the entire paper, which is called the constraint-first stance.

05:36.020 --> 05:37.420
The constraint-first stance.

05:37.600 --> 05:37.760
Right.

05:38.100 --> 05:41.680
It's a fundamental shift in how we try to understand complex systems.

05:42.300 --> 05:50.040
Instead of asking what a system is or what it's made of, you figure out its fundamental structure by rigorously defining what it is not permitted to do.

05:50.040 --> 05:52.260
Okay, that makes me think of thermodynamics.

05:52.820 --> 05:53.220
Exactly.

05:53.720 --> 06:00.300
Because the laws of thermodynamics don't really give you a microscopic description of what a cloud of gas is doing at any given moment.

06:00.380 --> 06:00.920
No, they don't.

06:01.320 --> 06:03.900
They just lay down these hard, unbreakable rules.

06:04.040 --> 06:09.080
They tell you, hey, I don't care what kind of machine you invent, you cannot build a perpetual motion device.

06:09.160 --> 06:09.340
Right.

06:09.520 --> 06:12.360
And you cannot decrease the total entropy in a closed system.

06:12.520 --> 06:15.980
What's fascinating here is that thermodynamics is the perfect parallel.

06:16.660 --> 06:18.600
The constraints generate the structure.

06:18.600 --> 06:22.440
And we see the exact same thing in computer programming with type systems.

06:22.780 --> 06:22.980
Oh, really?

06:23.240 --> 06:23.420
Yeah.

06:23.860 --> 06:26.900
A type system doesn't tell a program what logic to execute.

06:27.140 --> 06:32.480
It simply dictates which expressions are absolutely forbidden from ever being formed.

06:32.620 --> 06:34.320
It walls off the impossible.

06:34.700 --> 06:34.980
I see.

06:35.200 --> 06:44.480
So if we apply this constraint-first view to the idea of making a distinction, you know, leaving that scar on reality, we arrive at the concept of admissibility.

06:44.900 --> 06:45.460
Admissibility.

06:45.700 --> 06:45.900
Right.

06:45.900 --> 06:50.980
If history cannot be undone, what are you actually licensed to commit to that history?

06:51.900 --> 06:56.300
And to figure that out, the paper breaks down availability into these four distinct tiers.

06:56.540 --> 06:56.800
Yes.

06:56.980 --> 07:01.140
And I want to spend some time here because this is the backbone of everything we're going to talk about today.

07:01.840 --> 07:07.060
The four hurdles a potential action has to clear before it is allowed to become a permanent record.

07:07.060 --> 07:09.480
It's essentially a rigorous filtration system.

07:09.680 --> 07:09.900
Right.

07:10.220 --> 07:11.200
Walk us through these tiers.

07:11.400 --> 07:12.020
What's the first one?

07:12.300 --> 07:17.040
The foundation, the absolute lowest bar, is that a thing is describable.

07:17.720 --> 07:18.240
Describable.

07:18.380 --> 07:18.540
Yeah.

07:18.880 --> 07:23.900
This just means it is a well-formed expression in whatever language or logic system you're using.

07:24.020 --> 07:25.720
So it's grammatically correct.

07:25.720 --> 07:26.160
Okay.

07:26.520 --> 07:28.900
But grammatically correct doesn't mean real.

07:29.220 --> 07:30.220
No, not at all.

07:30.340 --> 07:33.760
Like I can describe a square circle or a married bachelor.

07:34.300 --> 07:38.400
The words fit together perfectly, but they point to an impossible physical reality.

07:38.640 --> 07:39.120
Precisely.

07:39.500 --> 07:42.700
Which is why we need the second tier, which is constructible.

07:43.660 --> 07:44.100
Constructible.

07:44.100 --> 07:45.920
So we're moving from words to actions.

07:46.360 --> 07:46.680
Exactly.

07:47.320 --> 07:48.500
It's a significant step up.

07:49.000 --> 07:55.200
For something to be constructible, a tangible step-by-step procedure must actually exist to make the thing,

07:55.720 --> 08:00.040
using the physical or computational resources the system currently possesses.

08:00.180 --> 08:01.880
So you aren't just describing it.

08:02.040 --> 08:02.280
Right.

08:02.460 --> 08:05.640
You can actually build a machine to find it or create it.

08:05.960 --> 08:06.260
Okay.

08:06.420 --> 08:07.340
Let me make sure I'm following.

08:08.280 --> 08:10.760
Let's say I have a password cracking program.

08:10.760 --> 08:15.180
I can describe the idea of guessing every possible password until I get in.

08:15.500 --> 08:15.560
Right.

08:15.680 --> 08:16.340
That's tier one.

08:16.720 --> 08:17.240
Describable.

08:17.540 --> 08:22.600
Then tier two is that I actually write the script that runs through the dictionary a million times a second.

08:22.960 --> 08:24.500
I have constructed the search.

08:24.640 --> 08:24.920
Perfect.

08:25.120 --> 08:25.380
Yes.

08:25.480 --> 08:26.440
What's the third tier then?

08:26.580 --> 08:28.720
The third tier is verifiable.

08:29.520 --> 08:30.000
Verifiable.

08:30.380 --> 08:30.580
Right.

08:30.960 --> 08:32.800
This means you've constructed the process.

08:33.120 --> 08:34.520
It has produced an output.

08:34.960 --> 08:39.900
And now there is concrete evidence that a designated checker within the system will actually accept.

08:39.900 --> 08:42.400
It meets the constraints of the auditor.

08:43.060 --> 08:43.960
Wait, let me just jump in here.

08:44.060 --> 08:46.180
I'm trying to wrap my head around this difference.

08:46.360 --> 08:46.480
Sure.

08:46.980 --> 08:55.240
If my password script is constructible and it eventually finds the password, shouldn't it automatically be verifiable?

08:55.540 --> 08:57.240
Like why are those two different tiers?

08:57.800 --> 08:58.820
You know, you'd think so.

08:59.060 --> 09:04.640
But the math here proves that possible in theory is entirely useless if it's not verifiable in context.

09:04.860 --> 09:05.300
What do you mean?

09:05.300 --> 09:10.480
Well, the author uses a great conceptual proof for the gap between constructible and verifiable.

09:11.380 --> 09:16.220
Imagine a system checker that has a strict time limit or like a length limit.

09:16.400 --> 09:16.560
Okay.

09:16.820 --> 09:20.480
It will only accept a proof if it can be read in, say, 10 minutes.

09:21.040 --> 09:27.500
Now, you could theoretically run an exhaustive search that eventually finds a massive, sprawling mathematical truth.

09:27.640 --> 09:27.860
Right.

09:27.940 --> 09:29.060
So that makes it constructible.

09:29.060 --> 09:29.660
Exactly.

09:30.660 --> 09:38.620
But if the absolute shortest possible way to explain that truth to the checker takes a thousand years to read, your checker will simply time out.

09:38.700 --> 09:39.220
It will fail.

09:39.500 --> 09:40.080
Oh, wow.

09:40.340 --> 09:42.480
So the truth exists and you found it.

09:42.820 --> 09:47.400
But because of the physical limits of the system, it is functionally unverifiable.

09:47.740 --> 09:48.040
Yes.

09:48.520 --> 09:50.380
It's like finding the cure for a disease.

09:50.380 --> 09:55.700
But the formula is so complex that you die of old age before you can finish writing it down on the whiteboard.

09:55.960 --> 09:57.820
That is a perfect analogy.

09:57.820 --> 10:08.260
There are actual problems in computer science, like proofs related to the pigeonhole principle, where the shortest possible resolution is astronomically long.

10:08.900 --> 10:13.400
So a system can compute it, but it cannot verify it within any reasonable bounds.

10:13.740 --> 10:14.100
Exactly.

10:14.700 --> 10:18.780
And that brings us to the final ultimate tier, committable.

10:19.020 --> 10:19.560
Committable.

10:19.940 --> 10:20.780
The finish line.

10:21.340 --> 10:21.720
Right.

10:22.180 --> 10:26.500
Let's say you have described it, constructed it, and the checker has completely verified it.

10:26.500 --> 10:29.480
The last hurdle is the gate context.

10:30.120 --> 10:30.660
Which means what?

10:31.260 --> 10:41.560
Do you actually have the authorization, the policy clearance, and the resources to license this verified result into the permanent, irreversible history of the system?

10:41.900 --> 10:42.400
Oh, I see.

10:42.500 --> 10:42.700
Okay.

10:42.820 --> 10:43.940
Let's say I go to the bank.

10:44.480 --> 10:46.520
I can verify my identity to the bank.

10:46.520 --> 10:51.920
I can prove cryptographically that a transaction of a million dollars is mathematically sound.

10:52.180 --> 10:52.280
Right.

10:52.780 --> 10:57.300
But if I don't actually have a million dollars in my account, the bank is not going to commit that transaction.

10:57.380 --> 10:57.860
Exactly.

10:58.380 --> 11:04.380
Or to bring it back to your opening example, you can draft the furious email that makes it constructible.

11:04.580 --> 11:04.740
Yeah.

11:04.880 --> 11:07.500
You run it through spellcheck, so it's verifiable.

11:07.500 --> 11:13.740
But if you rip the power cord out before the server authorizes the send, it is not committable.

11:14.080 --> 11:15.400
It never enters history.

11:15.980 --> 11:17.940
So these aren't just abstract logical quirks.

11:18.040 --> 11:22.540
They track genuine catastrophic failures in engineering and in human life.

11:22.800 --> 11:23.160
Absolutely.

11:23.480 --> 11:27.480
It's basically an elaborate, mathematically rigorous way of saying talk is cheap.

11:27.640 --> 11:28.700
That's a great way to put it.

11:28.700 --> 11:40.220
Because you can have a specification you can describe but can't implement, an implementation you can construct but can't audit, and an audited, verified result that you simply don't have the authority to act upon.

11:40.540 --> 11:40.680
Right.

11:41.420 --> 11:45.740
Admissibility isn't about finding truth in some omniscient philosophical sense.

11:45.860 --> 11:51.540
It is strictly about what a system is currently licensed to permanently commit to its own history.

11:51.540 --> 11:59.000
Okay, so a distinction is only consequential if it survives this brutal four-tier gauntlet and actually gets committed.

11:59.300 --> 11:59.540
Yes.

11:59.880 --> 12:06.320
And once it's committed, it is irreversible, which perfectly sets up the next massive leap the research takes.

12:06.500 --> 12:07.340
It's a big leap.

12:07.960 --> 12:08.340
It is.

12:08.620 --> 12:14.740
Because if committing to a choice permanently alters history, how does the universe itself handle history?

12:15.220 --> 12:19.940
We're moving from the abstract logic of algorithms straight into the fundamental physics of time.

12:20.160 --> 12:20.600
We are.

12:20.600 --> 12:24.520
The text introduces a framework called the RSVP plenum.

12:24.700 --> 12:28.360
Which stands for Relativistic Scalar Vector Entropy Plenum.

12:28.480 --> 12:29.980
Yeah, it is quite the mouthful.

12:30.060 --> 12:30.920
Five times fast.

12:31.000 --> 12:32.000
Yeah, it is quite the mouthful.

12:32.420 --> 12:35.720
But it is a constraint-first physical research program.

12:35.920 --> 12:37.240
A research program.

12:37.240 --> 12:45.780
Yes, and I really want to emphasize that the author carefully calls it a research program, borrowing terminology from the philosopher of science, Imre Lakatos.

12:46.300 --> 12:47.380
Remind me about Lakatos.

12:47.640 --> 12:47.880
Sure.

12:47.880 --> 12:55.900
So Lakatos argued that science progresses through these programs, which have a hard core of foundational ideas that you never question.

12:55.900 --> 13:03.960
And that core is surrounded by a protective belt of auxiliary hypotheses and models that you can tweak when experiments fail.

13:04.160 --> 13:10.040
So the author is basically saying, look, I am not claiming this is the final empirical theory of quantum gravity.

13:10.100 --> 13:10.320
Right.

13:10.640 --> 13:16.020
I'm building a mathematical schema to show how irreversibility must function at a base level.

13:16.020 --> 13:16.740
Exactly.

13:17.200 --> 13:19.540
It's a highly responsible way to do theoretical work.

13:20.160 --> 13:23.100
They separate the mathematical proofs from the empirical claims.

13:23.360 --> 13:23.820
That makes sense.

13:24.340 --> 13:33.220
And the hard core of the RSVP program is the proposal that instead of thinking of particles moving through a pre-existing static grid of space-time...

13:33.220 --> 13:34.640
Like actors on an empty stage.

13:34.740 --> 13:34.940
Right.

13:35.320 --> 13:40.600
Instead of that, we should think of physical reality as a continuous medium, a plenum.

13:40.600 --> 13:46.820
And this medium, this plenum, it has three main dials or fields that define everything happening inside it.

13:47.440 --> 13:49.780
The text calls this the minimal schema.

13:50.000 --> 13:50.240
Right.

13:50.680 --> 13:52.740
Let's break those down for everyone listening.

13:52.780 --> 13:53.000
Okay.

13:53.080 --> 13:56.980
So the three fields are capacity, flow, and entropy density.

13:57.280 --> 13:59.200
Flow and entropy, I kind of get.

13:59.500 --> 13:59.740
Yeah.

13:59.880 --> 14:03.240
Flow, which is denoted as a vector field, is relatively intuitive.

14:03.680 --> 14:07.740
It describes how things move, how information and material transport through the medium.

14:08.120 --> 14:09.440
And entropy density.

14:09.440 --> 14:19.220
Entropy density is a measure of how much local configurations have degraded, how much information has been smeared out, scrambled, and basically lost.

14:19.440 --> 14:23.060
But it's this capacity field that the paper leans on so heavily, right?

14:23.440 --> 14:23.920
Yes.

14:24.760 --> 14:32.200
Capacity, which is represented by the scalar field phi, is the most distinctive interpretive weight in the entire program.

14:32.340 --> 14:33.180
What does it actually do?

14:33.680 --> 14:38.180
The proposal is that phi measures the local budget of the plenum to support distinctions.

14:38.440 --> 14:39.000
So it's a budget.

14:39.000 --> 14:39.720
Exactly.

14:40.500 --> 14:45.760
Where capacity is high, the universe can maintain incredibly fine, detailed, delicate structure.

14:46.100 --> 14:50.020
Where capacity is low, structure gets aggressively smoothed away.

14:50.540 --> 14:54.300
Finer distinctions physically require more capacity to exist.

14:54.820 --> 14:55.620
I love that concept.

14:56.080 --> 15:01.140
It's literally the universe's hard drive space for keeping things distinct from one another.

15:01.200 --> 15:02.820
That's exactly what it is.

15:02.820 --> 15:06.700
If the universe runs out of capacity in a certain area, things just blur together.

15:07.240 --> 15:10.860
But, you know, the real mind bender for me was what they do with the entropy field.

15:11.000 --> 15:11.720
Oh, absolutely.

15:12.100 --> 15:22.160
They argue that time, the ticking clock that we all experience, the arrow pointing from the past to the future, it is not some background stage that events happen on.

15:22.160 --> 15:26.660
Time emerges from irreversible entropy production.

15:26.660 --> 15:26.960
Yeah.

15:27.200 --> 15:33.740
And if we connect this to the bigger picture, this touches on something called Landauer's principle, which is crucial for understanding the whole paper.

15:34.140 --> 15:35.280
Tell me about Landauer's principle.

15:35.280 --> 15:40.720
So, in 1961, a physicist named Rolf Landauer proved something astonishing.

15:41.240 --> 15:47.560
He showed that there is an unavoidable physical thermodynamic cost to erasing data.

15:47.740 --> 15:48.640
Wait, explain that.

15:49.100 --> 15:54.200
Are you saying just the act of deleting a file on my computer creates heat?

15:54.500 --> 15:55.740
Literally, yes.

15:55.980 --> 15:56.240
Wow.

15:56.660 --> 16:00.420
See, when you process information, you are physically moving electrons around.

16:00.420 --> 16:05.920
Now, if you do a computation where you keep every single piece of intermediate data...

16:05.920 --> 16:07.380
So, you never throw anything away?

16:07.540 --> 16:07.680
Right.

16:07.760 --> 16:08.620
You never delete a thing.

16:09.020 --> 16:13.860
You can theoretically run that computation forward and backward without dissipating any heat at all.

16:14.240 --> 16:15.500
It's called reversible computing.

16:15.940 --> 16:17.720
Because no distinctions are lost.

16:17.880 --> 16:18.240
Exactly.

16:18.760 --> 16:26.440
But the moment you merge two states together, say you have a variable that could be a 1 or a 0, and you forcefully reset it to 0.

16:26.760 --> 16:28.720
You've lost information about what it used to be.

16:28.720 --> 16:29.040
Yes.

16:29.400 --> 16:31.140
You have erased a distinction.

16:32.320 --> 16:38.080
And Landauer proved that that erasure must release a tiny, specific amount of heat into the environment.

16:38.540 --> 16:40.980
So, forgetting has a physical thermal cost.

16:41.300 --> 16:42.180
It produces entropy.

16:42.460 --> 16:42.700
Yes.

16:43.080 --> 16:51.740
And Flyxion is taking this unbreakable link between logical irreversibility and physical irreversibility and scaling it up to the entire cosmos.

16:52.120 --> 16:53.440
That is wild.

16:53.640 --> 16:54.200
Isn't it?

16:54.200 --> 16:57.640
The universe computes, but it also forgets.

16:58.240 --> 17:00.480
It constantly merges states together.

17:00.940 --> 17:01.540
Things decay.

17:02.020 --> 17:03.400
Things crash into each other.

17:03.520 --> 17:04.480
Heat dissipates.

17:04.720 --> 17:04.940
Right.

17:05.200 --> 17:11.400
And the author is saying that this process of forgetting, this production of entropy, is the temporal order.

17:11.660 --> 17:11.980
Yes.

17:12.700 --> 17:19.940
The time that has passed for any object, its entropic age, is literally just the tally of all the entropy it has produced.

17:19.940 --> 17:23.020
So, temporal order is just reachability.

17:23.480 --> 17:23.820
Exactly.

17:24.600 --> 17:28.680
State B is later than state A purely because the universe produced entropy.

17:28.860 --> 17:30.980
It paid a physical price to get from A to B.

17:31.540 --> 17:35.500
And because of the constraints of thermodynamics, you cannot run that process backward.

17:35.880 --> 17:37.900
You can't un-dissipate the heat.

17:38.120 --> 17:38.480
No.

17:38.800 --> 17:39.040
No.

17:39.040 --> 17:43.560
The arrow of time is sharp only because the distinctions have been permanently merged.

17:44.220 --> 17:44.440
Okay, wait.

17:44.560 --> 17:47.400
Let me push on this because here is where my brain starts to melt a little bit.

17:47.520 --> 17:47.920
Go for it.

17:48.360 --> 17:51.500
What happens if the system hits maximum entropy?

17:51.720 --> 17:52.140
Ah, right.

17:52.480 --> 17:54.600
Because the paper talks about a heat death scenario.

17:55.000 --> 18:01.960
If time is just the accumulation of entropy and the universe eventually expands and cools and reaches a state of total equilibrium.

18:02.620 --> 18:04.520
Where entropy simply stops increasing.

18:04.520 --> 18:05.000
Right.

18:05.360 --> 18:06.900
Does time just stop?

18:07.580 --> 18:10.440
Even if, theoretically, a clock is floating in the void somewhere?

18:10.960 --> 18:12.720
Well, this raises an important question.

18:12.860 --> 18:17.160
And it is explicitly one of the open problems the author catalogs at the end of the text.

18:17.440 --> 18:18.700
What's the mathematical answer?

18:19.980 --> 18:22.780
Mathematically, according to this schema, yes.

18:23.200 --> 18:24.820
The entropic clock saturates.

18:25.500 --> 18:26.140
It stops.

18:26.340 --> 18:26.540
Yeah.

18:26.960 --> 18:31.740
In a late-time equilibrium, things might technically still be fluctuating at a quantum level.

18:31.740 --> 18:34.820
But they leave no irreversible marks.

18:35.640 --> 18:37.840
And without marks, there is no permanent record.

18:38.020 --> 18:38.140
Right.

18:38.640 --> 18:43.420
Without a permanent record of irreversible transitions, there is no temporal order.

18:43.940 --> 18:45.620
You lose the distinctions entirely.

18:46.000 --> 18:50.220
Time, as an emergent property of history, effectively ceases to exist.

18:50.620 --> 18:51.640
That is haunting.

18:51.980 --> 18:52.760
It really is.

18:53.140 --> 18:55.880
If nothing can leave a scar, time isn't passing.

18:56.520 --> 18:58.760
It's just a static, ahistorical blur.

18:58.760 --> 19:03.560
It forces us to realize that time isn't a container we live inside.

19:03.760 --> 19:07.860
It is the exhaust fume of the universe making irreversible decisions.

19:08.240 --> 19:10.140
The exhaust fume of decisions.

19:10.720 --> 19:10.980
Wow.

19:11.740 --> 19:12.000
Okay.

19:12.120 --> 19:15.000
So the universe organically bakes in irreversibility.

19:15.420 --> 19:18.000
It generates time by merging states and forgetting.

19:18.200 --> 19:18.700
We get that.

19:18.980 --> 19:19.140
Yeah.

19:19.300 --> 19:20.900
And we can't change the laws of physics.

19:21.400 --> 19:24.660
But we build artificial universes all the time.

19:25.040 --> 19:27.840
We build computer networks, financial systems, databases.

19:27.840 --> 19:28.400
Right.

19:28.860 --> 19:34.460
How do we engineer these artificial systems to make irreversible decisions safely in a universe

19:34.460 --> 19:35.900
that has no undo button?

19:36.440 --> 19:39.340
Well, we're moving from the cosmos into computer science now.

19:39.460 --> 19:39.900
Exactly.

19:40.540 --> 19:42.280
The architecture of consequence.

19:42.960 --> 19:47.840
The computational counterpart to this whole philosophy is something the author calls the

19:47.840 --> 19:48.960
sphere pop calculus.

19:49.520 --> 19:53.140
And it's practical realization, which is sphere pop OS.

19:53.360 --> 19:54.380
Sphere pop OS.

19:54.380 --> 20:00.260
It is an operating system architecture designed from the ground up specifically for admissible

20:00.260 --> 20:01.320
state transitions.

20:02.040 --> 20:04.860
And it essentially runs on a life cycle of six verbs, right?

20:05.000 --> 20:05.240
Yes.

20:05.580 --> 20:10.540
Pop, bind, transform, verify, collapse, and refuse.

20:11.080 --> 20:12.080
That's correct.

20:12.900 --> 20:17.420
And to understand why this architecture is so revolutionary, you have to look at how poorly

20:17.420 --> 20:18.780
we currently manage data.

20:19.000 --> 20:19.440
How so?

20:19.440 --> 20:22.340
Most modern systems just overwrite things.

20:23.000 --> 20:26.760
When you log into your bank and change your address, the database just deletes the old

20:26.760 --> 20:28.120
address and writes in the new one.

20:28.380 --> 20:28.480
Right.

20:28.920 --> 20:33.020
State has changed, but the local history of that change is obliterated.

20:33.280 --> 20:33.800
Exactly.

20:34.840 --> 20:36.580
Sphere pop refuses to operate that way.

20:36.820 --> 20:42.800
It enforces a strict architectural separation between the working state and the evidentiary log.

20:42.800 --> 20:48.460
Okay, so if I'm understanding this correctly, it's basically formalizing the idea of a draft

20:48.460 --> 20:48.820
folder.

20:49.160 --> 20:49.980
That's a great analogy.

20:50.420 --> 20:50.600
Okay.

20:50.880 --> 20:53.240
Let's go back to our angry email from the beginning of the show.

20:54.020 --> 20:58.600
If I am furious at my boss, I don't just start altering the real world immediately.

20:59.040 --> 21:00.880
I open a new isolated window.

21:01.320 --> 21:02.380
That verb is pop.

21:02.820 --> 21:03.080
Okay.

21:03.080 --> 21:05.780
You've popped a provisional sphere into existence.

21:05.960 --> 21:06.460
Okay, pop.

21:06.460 --> 21:10.460
And then I attach my login credentials to it.

21:10.720 --> 21:11.480
That's bind.

21:11.960 --> 21:14.000
I brought my authorization into the sphere.

21:14.500 --> 21:14.600
Right.

21:15.300 --> 21:19.260
Then transform is me actually typing out my angry thoughts.

21:19.700 --> 21:21.940
I am computing the provisional state.

21:22.500 --> 21:23.380
I can read the email.

21:23.820 --> 21:26.940
I can see how devastating it is, but it's totally contained.

21:27.300 --> 21:27.900
Exactly.

21:27.900 --> 21:33.940
You are freely mutating data, but you have not altered the committed world state yet.

21:34.220 --> 21:37.720
The system is holding this provisional reality inside a private bubble.

21:38.480 --> 21:38.600
Okay.

21:38.880 --> 21:43.600
So then I run a spell check, or maybe the company has an HR filter that scans for profanity.

21:43.900 --> 21:45.180
That's verify.

21:45.600 --> 21:45.880
Yep.

21:46.000 --> 21:47.320
And then let's say it passes the check.

21:47.880 --> 21:49.120
This very moment, I have a choice.

21:49.600 --> 21:50.820
I can hit refuse.

21:51.400 --> 21:52.420
I can delete the draft.

21:52.580 --> 21:52.760
Right.

21:52.760 --> 21:57.560
And if I do that, the committed state of the world, my employment, my relationship with

21:57.560 --> 21:59.960
my boss, it remains completely untouched.

22:00.460 --> 22:00.820
It does.

22:01.440 --> 22:03.880
But SpherePop OS does something unique here.

22:04.340 --> 22:07.460
It keeps a permanent log that you almost sent it.

22:07.860 --> 22:08.980
It logs the refuse.

22:09.460 --> 22:11.120
Why log something that didn't happen?

22:11.680 --> 22:14.180
Because the refusal itself is a historical event.

22:14.860 --> 22:20.240
The fact that you spent computational resources proposing a state and then rejected it is vital

22:20.240 --> 22:21.400
evidentiary data.

22:21.400 --> 22:22.360
Oh, wow.

22:22.760 --> 22:24.700
And what if you don't hit refuse?

22:25.540 --> 22:29.120
Well, if I hit collapse, that is the point of no return.

22:29.420 --> 22:29.640
Yes.

22:30.040 --> 22:36.180
Collapse is the only verb in the entire operating system that actually changes the global committed

22:36.180 --> 22:36.980
world state.

22:37.400 --> 22:42.900
The email sends, the inbox registers a new message, and a permanent collapse entry is

22:42.900 --> 22:43.720
appended to the log.

22:44.100 --> 22:46.300
The analogy holds up beautifully.

22:46.300 --> 22:52.160
The author mathematically proves that refuse leaves the committed state untouched, while

22:52.160 --> 22:54.980
collapse acts as the boundary of irreversibility.

22:55.660 --> 22:56.480
That makes total sense.

22:57.160 --> 23:02.360
But the true genius, the real integrity of this architecture, is how that evidentiary log

23:02.360 --> 23:03.460
is actually constructed.

23:03.460 --> 23:05.240
It relies on two rules.

23:05.920 --> 23:08.220
Hash-linked logging and acyclic reference.

23:08.720 --> 23:08.920
Okay.

23:09.140 --> 23:09.980
Let's break those down.

23:10.520 --> 23:11.260
Hash-linking.

23:12.400 --> 23:14.860
I've heard that term used with blockchains and cryptocurrencies.

23:15.180 --> 23:16.360
Is it the same idea here?

23:16.920 --> 23:18.900
It uses similar cryptographic principles.

23:19.040 --> 23:19.240
Yes.

23:19.740 --> 23:25.820
Every single entry in the SpherePop log contains a cryptographic hash, a unique mathematical fingerprint

23:25.820 --> 23:28.260
of the entry that immediately preceded it.

23:28.980 --> 23:30.340
So it creates an unbreakable chain?

23:30.680 --> 23:31.080
Exactly.

23:31.340 --> 23:32.560
It makes the log tamper evident.

23:33.200 --> 23:34.240
What does that mean in practice?

23:34.800 --> 23:39.160
Well, if a malicious actor, or even just a system error, tries to go back in time and

23:39.160 --> 23:45.120
alter the log of your drafted email, the mathematical fingerprint of every single subsequent entry will

23:45.120 --> 23:49.380
instantly break, blaring a siren that the history has been tampered with.

23:49.840 --> 23:52.660
It's like sealing an envelope with a wax stamp.

23:52.760 --> 23:54.220
If someone opens it, you know.

23:54.220 --> 23:56.120
Great way to visualize it.

23:56.740 --> 23:58.440
But what is acyclic reference?

23:59.140 --> 24:03.380
Acyclic reference means that an entry in the log can only refer to things that happened

24:03.380 --> 24:04.320
before it was created.

24:04.540 --> 24:05.700
It cannot point forward.

24:06.240 --> 24:07.720
Well, I mean, that seems obvious, right?

24:07.800 --> 24:09.940
How could a computer log reference the future?

24:10.340 --> 24:16.320
It sounds obvious to a human experiencing linear time, but in computer science, allowing

24:16.320 --> 24:20.100
a data structure to reference itself or to reference something that is referencing

24:20.100 --> 24:23.420
it back, it creates massive paradoxes.

24:23.420 --> 24:24.840
Oh, like infinite loops.

24:25.000 --> 24:25.320
Exactly.

24:25.680 --> 24:27.900
It's the root of countless system crashes.

24:28.800 --> 24:32.020
The author insists that the commit log must point backward.

24:32.540 --> 24:36.320
The record of an action, the witness evidence, points to the commitment.

24:36.980 --> 24:41.060
But the commitment itself does not contain a reference to the witness evidence that will

24:41.060 --> 24:42.340
eventually be filed about it.

24:42.560 --> 24:42.920
Right.

24:42.920 --> 24:44.980
The action is what it is.

24:44.980 --> 24:47.040
The evidence is filed afterward.

24:48.140 --> 24:50.780
This separates the action from the record of the action.

24:51.480 --> 24:53.820
So the committed state of the world is just a projection.

24:54.620 --> 24:59.940
If you only look at the final committed state, like the sent email, you lose the entire messy

24:59.940 --> 25:01.040
history of how it got there.

25:01.300 --> 25:01.520
Yes.

25:01.740 --> 25:05.060
You lose the drafts, the hesitations, the failed verifications.

25:05.060 --> 25:08.040
And this framework scales up, right?

25:08.440 --> 25:11.200
What happens when you have a million people using SpherePop at once?

25:11.760 --> 25:14.740
Well, the text introduces coordination frameworks to handle that.

25:15.040 --> 25:17.400
And the most complex of these is called Tartan.

25:17.880 --> 25:18.140
Right.

25:18.300 --> 25:23.580
Tartan explores how local tiles, you know, local states, interacting only with their immediate

25:23.580 --> 25:26.620
neighbors, how they can create a globally coherent pattern.

25:26.620 --> 25:32.440
Think about a massive grid, where each square is its own little SpherePop system making local

25:32.440 --> 25:32.940
decisions.

25:33.440 --> 25:38.160
Can these local, bounded rules guarantee that the entire grid won't eventually tear itself

25:38.160 --> 25:38.520
apart?

25:38.920 --> 25:43.140
So it's like asking if a bunch of ants, who can only see a few inches in front of them

25:43.140 --> 25:47.980
and only follow local pheromone trails, can reliably build a perfectly structured colony

25:47.980 --> 25:48.720
without a blueprint.

25:49.360 --> 25:51.060
That's an excellent way to visualize it.

25:51.540 --> 25:56.320
And the text relies on a concept called Wang Tiles to prove a devastating point about this.

25:56.320 --> 25:57.200
Wang Tiles.

25:57.840 --> 25:58.300
What are those?

25:58.900 --> 26:00.860
Think of a Wang Tile like a puzzle piece.

26:01.140 --> 26:05.040
But instead of interlocking shapes, it's just a square with colored edges.

26:05.240 --> 26:05.480
Okay.

26:05.680 --> 26:09.840
The only rule is that you can only place a tile next to another tile if their touching

26:09.840 --> 26:11.360
edges have the exact same color.

26:11.720 --> 26:13.220
Sounds like a fun, simple game.

26:13.740 --> 26:17.300
It is, until you ask a computer a specific question.

26:18.000 --> 26:22.820
Given this specific set of colored tiles, can you cover an infinitely large floor without

26:22.820 --> 26:24.860
ever breaking the color matching rule?

26:24.860 --> 26:30.260
I feel like a modern supercomputer could easily calculate a definitive yes or no for that.

26:30.880 --> 26:31.820
You'd think so.

26:32.160 --> 26:36.800
But the math proves that for certain sets of tiles, this problem, which is known as the

26:36.800 --> 26:40.000
domino problem, is mathematically undecidable.

26:40.800 --> 26:41.200
Undecidable.

26:41.380 --> 26:41.620
Yes.

26:41.780 --> 26:44.340
No algorithm can ever give you a guaranteed answer.

26:44.680 --> 26:48.800
You just have to start laying tiles and hope you don't paint yourself into a corner a million

26:48.800 --> 26:49.560
miles down the line.

26:49.860 --> 26:50.100
Right.

26:50.100 --> 26:51.180
Let me process that.

26:51.980 --> 26:59.500
So even with this incredibly rigorous, mathematically perfect SpherePop OS, with its cryptographic

26:59.500 --> 27:06.300
hashes and strict verification gates, you can prove that a single action is safe.

27:06.500 --> 27:12.800
But you cannot guarantee that a massively complex network of them won't just suddenly spin out

27:12.800 --> 27:14.340
into emergent chaos.

27:14.340 --> 27:16.000
That is exactly the consequence.

27:16.800 --> 27:18.360
You can certify local safety.

27:18.580 --> 27:21.940
You cannot compute a global guarantee of emergent behavior.

27:22.260 --> 27:23.800
The math literally forbids it.

27:24.060 --> 27:25.940
That is deeply unsettling.

27:26.640 --> 27:30.140
We build these vast interconnected systems all the time.

27:30.260 --> 27:34.960
The global stock market, autonomous driving networks, the internet itself.

27:35.160 --> 27:35.360
Yeah.

27:35.360 --> 27:41.100
And at a fundamental level, we are just crossing our fingers that the local rules don't eventually

27:41.100 --> 27:42.380
produce a global paradox?

27:43.080 --> 27:44.020
It's a sobering thought.

27:44.600 --> 27:45.220
It really is.

27:45.280 --> 27:49.420
And speaking of vast systems, I have a practical problem with SpherePop.

27:49.660 --> 27:50.160
What's that?

27:50.740 --> 27:56.920
If you have a hash-linked log that records every single draft, every single refusal, every tiny

27:56.920 --> 28:00.900
action, forever, that log is going to get infinitely large.

28:01.300 --> 28:02.380
It absolutely is.

28:02.380 --> 28:05.200
A record that never forgets grows without bound.

28:05.320 --> 28:08.220
It will eventually consume all available physical memory.

28:08.660 --> 28:10.480
So you have to compact it, right?

28:10.540 --> 28:15.280
At some point, you have to take a giant chunk of the past, calculate the final committed state

28:15.280 --> 28:20.920
it resulted in, save that state, and throw away the messy, infinitely large log of how you

28:20.920 --> 28:21.320
got there.

28:21.520 --> 28:21.980
You do.

28:22.420 --> 28:25.260
But by doing that, you violate Landauer's principle again.

28:25.380 --> 28:25.900
Oh, right.

28:26.120 --> 28:27.140
You merge distinctions.

28:27.560 --> 28:29.000
You throw away the refusals.

28:29.180 --> 28:31.100
You lose the granular evidence.

28:31.100 --> 28:36.400
You are essentially trading evidentiary accountability for working state efficiency.

28:37.140 --> 28:42.980
And this physical limitation, this fact that infinite logs are physically impossible and

28:42.980 --> 28:48.580
compaction is just inevitable, it transitions brilliantly into the fourth section of the paper.

28:48.720 --> 28:49.700
It's my favorite section.

28:50.080 --> 28:53.640
Because computer logs are a luxury of silicon and massive data centers.

28:53.640 --> 29:00.560
Biological systems, human minds, we do not have the capacity to store a perfect, append-only

29:00.560 --> 29:01.600
log of our lives.

29:01.700 --> 29:02.340
We really don't.

29:02.640 --> 29:05.920
We have incredibly strict space constraints inside our skulls.

29:05.920 --> 29:10.720
So when you can't keep a perfect log, how do you know what happened?

29:10.720 --> 29:13.920
And more importantly, how do you know who you are?

29:14.520 --> 29:18.420
This is where the paper transitions from silicon to cognitive psychology.

29:18.660 --> 29:23.140
It introduces a framework for memory and identity called MEM-8.

29:23.260 --> 29:24.120
MEM-8.

29:24.320 --> 29:24.520
Right.

29:24.520 --> 29:32.080
And it starts with a vital fundamental distinction between two concepts, preservation versus reachability.

29:32.600 --> 29:37.040
Okay, unpack this for us because this fundamentally changed how I view my own brain.

29:37.260 --> 29:41.600
We culturally tend to think of human memory like a video file stored on a hard drive or

29:41.600 --> 29:42.480
a book in a library.

29:42.700 --> 29:45.880
Yeah, if you want to remember something, you just walk down the aisle, find the book, and

29:45.880 --> 29:46.220
open it.

29:46.360 --> 29:46.700
Exactly.

29:47.340 --> 29:49.980
That implies the original material is preserved.

29:49.980 --> 29:53.800
It is sitting there, whole and intact, waiting to be accessed.

29:54.060 --> 29:54.160
Right.

29:54.600 --> 29:58.720
But MEM-8 argues that biological memory operates on reachability.

29:59.040 --> 29:59.560
Reachability.

30:00.100 --> 30:01.040
What's the difference?

30:01.420 --> 30:07.780
Reachability means the system possesses a process to generate a target output given current conditions,

30:08.000 --> 30:12.640
even if the original material isn't stored in full anywhere in the system.

30:12.920 --> 30:16.820
To prove this, the author introduces the constraint of the 8-byte memory cell.

30:17.440 --> 30:18.520
And this blew my mind.

30:18.520 --> 30:20.200
It's mathematically undeniable.

30:20.580 --> 30:20.660
Yeah.

30:21.000 --> 30:26.300
By mathematical proof, a single 8-byte cell, which is tiny, I mean, it's literally just

30:26.300 --> 30:27.280
64 bits of data.

30:27.620 --> 30:29.920
A text message is vastly larger than that.

30:30.000 --> 30:30.560
Way larger.

30:30.920 --> 30:34.540
It cannot possibly hold a complex, rich, multisensory memory.

30:35.180 --> 30:39.220
The counting bound, just the sheer mathematics of information theory, completely forbids it.

30:39.480 --> 30:39.880
It does.

30:40.160 --> 30:45.820
So if your brain is outputting a rich, complex memory from an 8-byte encoded cell, the information

30:45.820 --> 30:47.240
is not coming from the cell itself.

30:47.240 --> 30:47.740
It cannot.

30:47.960 --> 30:49.060
It must be reconstructed.

30:49.420 --> 30:52.640
The vivid memory you experience is a joint product of three things.

30:53.120 --> 30:58.040
The tiny, heavily compressed cell, the cue that triggered the memory, and the present

30:58.040 --> 31:00.100
context of the observer doing the remembering.

31:00.580 --> 31:02.260
I want you listening to try something right now.

31:02.760 --> 31:04.860
Think about your own earliest memory.

31:05.240 --> 31:07.740
The one you are absolutely sure is real.

31:07.800 --> 31:08.460
We all have one.

31:08.460 --> 31:08.900
Yeah.

31:09.180 --> 31:10.500
Maybe it's your 10th birthday.

31:10.980 --> 31:12.160
You can smell the cake.

31:12.480 --> 31:14.140
You can see the color of the balloons.

31:14.360 --> 31:15.900
You remember the song playing in the background.

31:16.580 --> 31:20.820
This paper is mathematically suggesting that you didn't actually store a video of that party.

31:20.900 --> 31:21.380
No, you didn't.

31:21.740 --> 31:24.840
Your brain stored a tiny 8-byte zip file.

31:25.520 --> 31:29.760
And right now, as you try to remember it, your brain is basically hallucinating all those

31:29.760 --> 31:34.340
rich details based on your current mood and whatever cue prompted you to think about it.

31:34.340 --> 31:36.920
It means memory isn't a vault at all.

31:37.060 --> 31:38.540
It's a resonant reconstruction.

31:39.540 --> 31:44.680
The text connects this to Endel-Tulving's psychological principle of encoding specificity

31:44.680 --> 31:47.720
and Frederick Bartlett's work on reconstructive memory.

31:48.380 --> 31:50.280
So you do not retrieve a memory.

31:50.640 --> 31:53.720
You rebuild it on the fly every single time.

31:53.820 --> 31:54.480
Every single time.

31:54.800 --> 31:58.240
And the author models this using a mechanism called interference gating.

31:58.440 --> 31:58.700
Yes.

31:59.040 --> 32:00.160
The phase gate mechanism.

32:00.760 --> 32:02.680
This was such a cool way to visualize it.

32:02.680 --> 32:04.940
And let's use an analogy for this.

32:05.080 --> 32:05.500
Go for it.

32:05.720 --> 32:08.440
Imagine your brain is an old analog radio.

32:08.920 --> 32:13.560
And every single memory trace you have is a different radio station broadcasting at a

32:13.560 --> 32:14.620
specific frequency.

32:14.800 --> 32:15.580
Okay, I'm with you.

32:15.920 --> 32:20.220
And a cue like a smell, a question, or a photograph.

32:20.980 --> 32:24.480
That cue is you turning the dial to try and find a specific station.

32:24.720 --> 32:25.100
Exactly.

32:25.400 --> 32:29.900
When you try to remember something, you are opening a gate or a receiver on that radio.

32:29.900 --> 32:35.360
Now, if you make the receiver very broad, a wide gate, you will almost certainly catch

32:35.360 --> 32:38.820
the station you're looking for, even if your hand slips on the dial or the cue is a bit

32:38.820 --> 32:39.200
noisy.

32:39.760 --> 32:42.980
But because the receiver is so wide, you're going to pick up a ton of static.

32:43.200 --> 32:43.420
Right.

32:43.680 --> 32:47.620
And worse, you're going to hear two or three other stations bleeding through at the exact

32:47.620 --> 32:48.200
same time.

32:48.880 --> 32:54.420
The brain takes all that overlapping noise and desperately stitches it together into a single

32:54.420 --> 32:55.380
coherent narrative.

32:55.380 --> 32:57.340
And those are false memories.

32:57.700 --> 32:57.980
Well.

32:58.240 --> 33:01.360
That's you remembering your cousin being at a party they never actually attended.

33:01.720 --> 33:04.580
Because the cousin's station bled into the party station.

33:04.740 --> 33:06.160
That makes so much sense.

33:06.580 --> 33:11.920
The expected number of non-target traces, these competitors, it increases linearly with the

33:11.920 --> 33:12.580
width of the gate.

33:13.120 --> 33:13.480
Okay.

33:13.880 --> 33:16.000
But what if I don't want false memories?

33:16.340 --> 33:21.060
Well, if you try to fix this by making the gate ultra narrow, trying to get perfect,

33:21.060 --> 33:26.020
pristine clarity with no false memories, you introduce a completely new vulnerability.

33:26.480 --> 33:26.740
Which is?

33:27.400 --> 33:32.100
If your cue isn't absolutely mathematically perfect, if your hand is even a millimeter

33:32.100 --> 33:34.760
off on the radio dial, you get absolute silence.

33:34.920 --> 33:36.260
You miss the target entirely.

33:36.400 --> 33:37.780
The memory is unreachable.

33:37.780 --> 33:41.320
So it's a fundamental trade-off of biological storage.

33:41.840 --> 33:42.120
It is.

33:42.440 --> 33:47.800
And this leads directly to a concept that might be the most philosophically aggressive part

33:47.800 --> 33:48.740
of the entire paper.

33:49.600 --> 33:52.460
Apparent versus demonstrable continuity.

33:52.840 --> 33:53.100
Yeah.

33:53.240 --> 33:57.900
The author formally proves that plausible reconstruction does not establish preservation.

33:57.900 --> 34:03.360
Just because you can reconstruct a perfectly coherent, highly detailed, very convincing

34:03.360 --> 34:08.460
memory of your 10th birthday does not mean you preserve the actual historical event.

34:08.760 --> 34:09.940
Let's contextualize this.

34:10.220 --> 34:15.680
If I have a heavily pixelated compressed photo from the 1990s and I run it through a modern

34:15.680 --> 34:20.420
AI upscaler, it might spit out a beautiful photorealistic 4K image.

34:20.520 --> 34:20.700
Sure.

34:20.960 --> 34:23.400
That is apparent continuity.

34:23.680 --> 34:24.540
It looks real.

34:24.940 --> 34:26.020
It makes logical sense.

34:26.020 --> 34:32.000
But demonstrable continuity requires an admissible evidenced chain of transitions.

34:32.440 --> 34:38.160
I have to be able to prove step-by-step, using spherepop level hash logs, how I got from

34:38.160 --> 34:40.300
the original event to the current state.

34:40.460 --> 34:45.420
Which links directly back to Landauer's principle and the data processing inequality.

34:45.760 --> 34:45.980
Right.

34:46.200 --> 34:48.280
Distinctions lost early are not recovered.

34:48.960 --> 34:54.520
When your brain compacted the memory of your 10th birthday to save space, it merged states.

34:54.520 --> 34:57.060
It threw away distinctions permanently.

34:57.680 --> 35:00.160
So you can never truly regenerate that lost data.

35:00.300 --> 35:03.080
You can only simulate it, upscale it, hallucinate it.

35:03.560 --> 35:08.760
And the real danger is that the system, our brain, treats its own reconstructions as if

35:08.760 --> 35:11.240
they are certified, verifiable records.

35:11.500 --> 35:14.760
That is why eyewitness testimony is so notoriously unreliable.

35:14.760 --> 35:15.480
Exactly.

35:16.140 --> 35:22.300
The brain reconstructed a plausible candidate from 8 bytes of data, slapped a verified sticker

35:22.300 --> 35:25.400
on it, and bypassed the strict verification step.

35:26.080 --> 35:29.800
There is no cryptographic hash linking your memory to the actual past.

35:29.800 --> 35:33.260
We are walking around with a profound evidentiary deficit.

35:33.720 --> 35:34.340
We really are.

35:34.960 --> 35:37.500
And this forces a massive crisis for decision-making.

35:37.840 --> 35:37.960
Right.

35:38.320 --> 35:43.360
Because if our actions are permanent and irreversible, but our memory, our evidence of the past, is

35:43.360 --> 35:48.600
inherently lossy, reconstructive, and highly vulnerable to our present context, how do we

35:48.600 --> 35:50.180
ever decide it is safe to act?

35:50.500 --> 35:53.040
That is the ultimate pragmatic question.

35:53.260 --> 35:57.640
Which brings us to the fifth section of the research, decision and synthesis.

35:57.640 --> 35:59.480
Because we do act.

35:59.980 --> 36:03.580
We make decisions every single day based on this lossy, imperfect data.

36:03.760 --> 36:04.260
We have to.

36:04.960 --> 36:09.860
The paper introduces a mathematical model for this called the reversibility gate and the

36:09.860 --> 36:10.920
latency of evidence.

36:11.200 --> 36:15.340
And it starts by separating out a timeline that human beings normally blur all together

36:15.340 --> 36:15.980
into one moment.

36:16.780 --> 36:20.820
The author says there are three distinct times in any decision-making process.

36:21.300 --> 36:21.480
Okay.

36:21.840 --> 36:24.300
First, there is the time the evidence is observed.

36:24.540 --> 36:24.940
Observed.

36:25.460 --> 36:27.100
So the raw data exists in the world.

36:27.100 --> 36:27.580
Exactly.

36:28.040 --> 36:31.800
Second, there is the time the claim becomes decidable.

36:32.040 --> 36:32.480
Decidable.

36:32.880 --> 36:33.820
What does that mean?

36:34.140 --> 36:35.560
This means the evidence has matured.

36:35.940 --> 36:37.020
The noise has settled.

36:37.580 --> 36:39.800
The posterior probability has concentrated.

36:40.260 --> 36:43.020
And you have reached a specific tolerance of certainty.

36:43.180 --> 36:44.660
You basically know what's going on.

36:44.840 --> 36:45.020
Okay.

36:45.200 --> 36:47.300
So observed, then decidable.

36:47.300 --> 36:53.760
And third, there's the time an action is justified, which is the precise moment you are actually

36:53.760 --> 36:57.520
licensed to pull the trigger and commit a distinction to history.

36:57.520 --> 37:01.460
And the fascinating part is that these three times don't have to line up.

37:02.100 --> 37:07.520
The paper provides the actual math for the threshold of justification, which it calls P star.

37:07.620 --> 37:08.440
P star, right.

37:08.440 --> 37:11.480
This threshold is determined by two main opposing forces.

37:12.500 --> 37:19.800
The reversibility of the intervention, which is denoted by the Greek letter rho, and the cost of delay, denoted by kappa.

37:20.440 --> 37:21.340
Let's make this concrete.

37:22.000 --> 37:22.100
Okay.

37:22.260 --> 37:24.340
Consider a simple low-stakes decision.

37:24.340 --> 37:27.720
Like, I am deciding whether to carry an umbrella when I leave the house.

37:28.300 --> 37:29.980
That is a highly reversible decision.

37:30.280 --> 37:33.540
If I'm wrong and it doesn't rain, I just carry a light stick around for an hour.

37:33.820 --> 37:36.120
It costs you almost nothing to reverse the mistake.

37:36.320 --> 37:36.660
Exactly.

37:37.300 --> 37:40.540
So, the reversibility parameter, rho, is very high.

37:41.120 --> 37:46.660
Because it's easily reversible, the math says the threshold for action, P star, is incredibly low.

37:47.200 --> 37:49.720
I will act on tiny, flimsy evidence.

37:50.360 --> 37:52.960
I'll look at one slightly dark cloud and grab the umbrella.

37:52.960 --> 37:54.140
I don't need certainty.

37:54.560 --> 37:54.780
Yes.

37:55.320 --> 38:02.280
The math states that if an action is highly reversible, you are perfectly justified in acting before the claim is even decidable.

38:02.740 --> 38:06.360
You can leap before you look because climbing back up is easy.

38:06.900 --> 38:12.940
But if I'm deciding to get a permanent tattoo across my forehead, the reversibility is basically zero.

38:13.560 --> 38:17.820
The expected harm, if I get it wrong, or if I misspell it, is massive.

38:18.020 --> 38:18.620
Huge harm.

38:18.820 --> 38:21.820
So, the threshold shoots up to basically 100%.

38:21.820 --> 38:26.100
The action is only justified after the claim is totally decidable.

38:26.100 --> 38:30.520
I need rock-solid, mature evidence that this is a good idea.

38:30.820 --> 38:32.440
That's exactly how the gate functions.

38:32.880 --> 38:33.180
But wait.

38:33.660 --> 38:36.700
This model assumes that waiting for evidence is safe.

38:37.260 --> 38:40.600
What about that other parameter, kappa, the cost of delay?

38:40.600 --> 38:44.040
What if waiting to act is the irreversible harm?

38:44.760 --> 38:50.820
This is where the model earns its keep, because it mathematically maps out exactly that agonizing edge case.

38:50.820 --> 38:52.760
Let's take a medical example.

38:53.240 --> 38:53.520
Okay.

38:53.520 --> 38:56.260
Imagine a disease with a very long latency period.

38:56.640 --> 39:02.780
The latency of evidence is long, meaning it takes years to get good, solid data on whether the treatment works or not.

39:02.920 --> 39:03.100
Right.

39:03.440 --> 39:07.140
And the intervention, the treatment itself, is highly irreversible.

39:07.680 --> 39:13.200
According to the strict reversibility gate, the math says you should not act because you don't have mature evidence.

39:13.500 --> 39:18.220
But while you wait for the evidence to mature, the patient is accumulating irreversible harm.

39:18.220 --> 39:25.900
So the system paralyzes itself, the gate stays shut, and the patient suffers because the system demands certainty it can't have.

39:26.000 --> 39:26.240
Wow.

39:26.560 --> 39:26.920
Exactly.

39:27.700 --> 39:31.400
Unless you factor in kappa, the cost of inaction.

39:31.760 --> 39:32.700
The kappa parameter.

39:33.060 --> 39:33.360
Yes.

39:33.980 --> 39:42.260
The parameter kappa is the only mathematical force in this entire schema that can forcefully push the gate open in the absence of mature evidence.

39:42.580 --> 39:44.500
It overrides the need for certainty.

39:44.500 --> 39:50.640
The text explicitly warns against the human illusion that inaction is a costless, neutral state.

39:51.160 --> 39:53.980
Delay is itself an irreversible action.

39:54.400 --> 39:57.600
Because every second you wait, the universe generates entropy.

39:58.020 --> 39:58.980
Time moves forward.

39:59.500 --> 40:02.740
You are committing a distinction to history just by standing still.

40:03.300 --> 40:04.600
You can't pause the universe.

40:04.600 --> 40:14.260
So within the strict, hash-linked architecture of SpherePop, if you are an agent trying to make a decision, can you force the gate open?

40:14.720 --> 40:17.500
Can you act without evidence if the cost of delay is too high?

40:17.720 --> 40:18.100
You can.

40:18.820 --> 40:22.020
The author includes a corollary covering gated commitment.

40:22.480 --> 40:26.900
The system allows you to waive the evidentiary obligation and override the threshold.

40:27.260 --> 40:27.580
But?

40:27.580 --> 40:33.320
But, and this is the absolute crux of the philosophy, it is a logged, committed act.

40:33.680 --> 40:36.660
The system records permanently that you overrode the gate.

40:36.980 --> 40:39.060
You must own the override.

40:39.760 --> 40:40.280
That is brilliant.

40:40.900 --> 40:45.560
It doesn't physically stop you from leaping blind, but it forces you to sign a receipt saying,

40:45.920 --> 40:49.560
I am leaping without evidence, and I take responsibility for the fall.

40:50.120 --> 40:50.440
Yes.

40:50.440 --> 40:52.780
It holds you accountable to the history you're writing.

40:53.280 --> 40:56.040
You can't pretend three years later that you had the evidence all along.

40:56.400 --> 40:58.360
The ledger proves you acted on a waiver.

40:58.840 --> 41:01.120
And that brings us to the synthesis of the entire monograph.

41:01.800 --> 41:06.880
It really is a master class in tying seemingly disconnected fields together.

41:07.340 --> 41:08.140
It truly is.

41:08.600 --> 41:12.260
What this author has done is take one core, unshakable idea,

41:12.640 --> 41:15.700
that every time a system merges states and forgets a distinction,

41:16.340 --> 41:18.840
a permanent, irreversible step is taken.

41:18.840 --> 41:22.380
And they rigorously applied it to everything.

41:23.000 --> 41:23.280
Everything.

41:24.000 --> 41:29.160
The universe generates the arrow of time by aggressively forgetting microscopic distinctions.

41:29.760 --> 41:32.540
Computer operating systems manage catastrophic failure

41:32.540 --> 41:36.100
by meticulously isolating where distinctions are allowed to merge.

41:36.700 --> 41:39.380
The human memory hallucinates a narrative of continuity

41:39.380 --> 41:43.320
because it was forced to compress distinctions just to fit inside a skull.

41:43.740 --> 41:47.120
And decision theory is just the math of figuring out exactly

41:47.120 --> 41:52.280
when it's safe to merge a distinction in a world where you can never, ever go back.

41:52.280 --> 41:55.280
The rules of admissibility are universal constraints.

41:55.920 --> 41:57.600
You can only commit what you can verify,

41:58.260 --> 42:01.920
and you can only verify what you can actually construct within your physical limits.

42:02.480 --> 42:03.760
But there is one final thought,

42:04.160 --> 42:08.400
one last provocation in the text that we have to talk about before we go.

42:08.920 --> 42:09.740
Oh, this part.

42:10.120 --> 42:10.420
Yes.

42:10.780 --> 42:13.580
It's something that fundamentally shook my understanding of myself.

42:14.140 --> 42:16.720
It's the philosophical implications for identity,

42:17.060 --> 42:19.140
particularly in systems that branch.

42:19.140 --> 42:21.780
Yes, the limits of the account.

42:22.820 --> 42:26.380
We defined identity earlier through the lens of demonstrable continuity.

42:27.020 --> 42:31.100
You are considered you because there is an evidenced, admissible,

42:31.500 --> 42:35.960
unbroken chain of historical transitions from the you of yesterday to the you of today.

42:36.120 --> 42:37.700
It's a continuous line.

42:38.260 --> 42:40.240
But what happens if that line branches?

42:41.180 --> 42:43.220
We don't deal with this in biology much,

42:43.320 --> 42:45.880
but in digital systems we branch things all the time.

42:45.880 --> 42:47.160
We fork code bases.

42:47.620 --> 42:48.580
We copy files.

42:49.540 --> 42:50.980
Imagine a thought experiment.

42:52.040 --> 42:54.560
A teleporter malfunction that perfectly duplicates you.

42:55.320 --> 42:59.220
Or, 50 years from now, a fork in a digital consciousness upload.

42:59.780 --> 43:03.800
You step into the machine, and suddenly there is clone A and clone B.

43:04.440 --> 43:07.280
It completely breaks the underlying math of identity.

43:07.740 --> 43:08.400
And here is why.

43:08.400 --> 43:14.960
If you are perfectly duplicated, clone A and clone B both possess a perfectly evidenced,

43:15.220 --> 43:20.720
mathematically sound, hash-linked history stretching all the way back to the original person.

43:20.840 --> 43:24.540
They both have a legitimate claim to demonstrable continuity.

43:24.740 --> 43:24.900
Right.

43:25.040 --> 43:28.760
Because you can trace A back to the origin, and you can trace B back to the origin.

43:29.440 --> 43:33.520
But, and this is the fatal flaw, they do not have a history to each other.

43:33.820 --> 43:34.300
Wow.

43:34.300 --> 43:39.600
Because to trace a line from clone A to clone B, you would have to trace A's history backward

43:39.600 --> 43:42.500
to the moment of the split, and then forward to B.

43:42.660 --> 43:46.640
And going backward violates the fundamental law of irreversibility.

43:46.820 --> 43:47.480
You cannot go backward.

43:47.700 --> 43:48.080
Exactly.

43:48.940 --> 43:54.240
In mathematical terms, the directed relationship remains transitive, but it is no longer symmetric.

43:55.060 --> 43:59.480
The mathematical relation of being the same entity as completely breaks down

43:59.480 --> 44:01.680
the moment a system is capable of branching.

44:01.680 --> 44:08.600
If a system can branch, the very concept of the single unified you might just be a mathematical

44:08.600 --> 44:09.080
illusion.

44:09.780 --> 44:14.300
It's an illusion born entirely from the fact that biological constraints haven't allowed

44:14.300 --> 44:15.800
us to clone ourselves yet.

44:16.240 --> 44:21.580
We think we have a persistent self because we've never been forced to confront a fork in our own

44:21.580 --> 44:22.660
historical log.

44:23.040 --> 44:23.480
Exactly.

44:24.420 --> 44:30.280
Once we do, identity as a rigid concept might just evaporate into a decentralized network of

44:30.280 --> 44:30.560
branches.

44:30.560 --> 44:31.940
You aren't a person.

44:32.380 --> 44:35.140
You're just a temporary node in a propagating history.

44:35.600 --> 44:39.260
I want you, the listener, to just let that simmer for a bit today.

44:39.860 --> 44:45.780
It forces us to realize that our deepest, most cherished definitions of self are entirely reliant

44:45.780 --> 44:49.160
on the accidental physical constraints we currently live under.

44:49.700 --> 44:50.480
Remove the constraint.

44:51.120 --> 44:52.000
You remove the self.

44:52.000 --> 44:59.220
From furious email drafts and medical x-rays to the heat death of the universe and the mathematical

44:59.220 --> 45:02.580
illusion of the self, we covered some serious ground today.

45:02.960 --> 45:03.520
We really did.

45:04.000 --> 45:08.040
Thank you for joining us on this deep dive into distinction under constraint.

45:08.520 --> 45:13.920
Keep questioning the distinctions you commit to history because once they're logged, well,

45:13.920 --> 45:15.320
they are there for good.

45:15.700 --> 45:16.420
Catch you next time.

